nodecode-snapcompact is a rewrite of vimona3ds's vimona3ds/hermes-snapcompact:

  https://github.com/vimona3ds/hermes-snapcompact
  commit 8a274ca4a501e5661f1e2762150c4c3f99e374ce
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 johnytest (LICENSE)

Rewritten: what the plugin does to a conversation, as one tool and one
command. The idea is the same - old turns leave the text and become dense
bitmap pages a vision model reads back, with the newest turns kept verbatim -
and so are the reading rules the guide teaches the model (a grid columns
wide and rows tall, read left to right then top to bottom, no word wrap, a
run of spaces collapsing to one, a line continuing on the next row). The
grid geometry is preserved as the section's columns, rows and scale
settings; a page takes the shape the original tuned per provider only in
its pixels, since a page here is one 5x7 font at one scale rather than the
library's provider-aware shapes. The tool answers the paths of the frames it
wrote, their pixel size, the characters they carry, the tokens the same
words would have cost as text and the tokens the frames cost, which is the
original's own accounting; pages past max_frames are dropped from the oldest
end and counted, as the original counted dropped characters. Rendering is
this folder's own: a 5x7 bitmap font in font.lisp and a one-bit greyscale
PNG encoder in png.lisp, so there is no Bun, no npm package and no bridge to
install.

Left out: the Hermes context-engine interface itself - the 75% threshold,
should_compress, the summarize mode it falls back to and the /compact-mode
switch - because Nodecode evicts history instead of compacting it, so there
is no engine slot to occupy: the operator or the model decides what to
archive and calls the tool. Also left out are the Python serializer that
turned OpenAI-format messages into the original's ¶user:/¶ai:/¶think:/¶call:
scoped text (the tool takes the text, or a file holding it, as it is), the
@oh-my-pi/snapcompact renderer with its provider-aware frame shapes,
line-repeat and stopword dimming, and the automatic rewrite of the host's
config.yaml that pointed its context.engine at the plugin.
